---
/**
 * /redazione: the kit a volunteer hands to an AI before writing a post.
 *
 * The whole feature is a document and a copy button. WebMCP, an MCP server and
 * an API key were all considered and all rejected: what a volunteer needs is a
 * briefing they can paste into whatever chat window they already have, on a
 * free tier, with no browser support and nothing to install.
 *
 * ## Why a textarea and not a code block
 *
 * It is the no-JavaScript path. Without the script a volunteer can still tap
 * into the box, select all and copy, which a `<pre>` on a phone makes
 * miserable; and the box scrolls internally, so a thousand-word prompt does not
 * turn the page into a corridor. What you read in it is exactly what the model
 * receives, which is the point: no hidden system prompt, no surprises.
 *
 * ## Why it is noindex and out of the sitemap
 *
 * Same reason as /styleguide: it is a tool for the people who run the site, not
 * a page of the site. Keeping it on the real domain rather than in the repo is
 * deliberate, a volunteer with a phone and a locandina can reach a URL.
 */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import H2 from '../../components/H2.astro';
import { KINDS, linkPrompt, prompt } from '../../lib/authoring';

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://www.ventorelativo.it');

const blocks = KINDS.map((entry) => ({
  ...entry,
  text: prompt(site, entry.kind),
  short: linkPrompt(site, entry.kind),
}));
const keystatic = new URL('/keystatic', site).href;
const plainText = new URL('/redazione/istruzioni.txt', site).href;
const asJson = new URL('/redazione/contenuti.json', site).href;

/**
 * Where the prompt can be sent, chosen once for the whole page.
 *
 * One choice at the top rather than a row of buttons per section: which chat a
 * volunteer uses is a fact about them, not about what they are writing, and it
 * is remembered, so after the first visit the page has one button.
 *
 * The `?q=` carries the **short** prompt, the one naming `istruzioni.txt`: the
 * full text is 26,736 characters encoded and Google answers 400 to a Gemini URL
 * somewhere between 8k and 20k. The button also copies the full text, so a
 * model that cannot open the link (every free tier, and everything at all until
 * the cutover lifts `Disallow: /`) is one paste from having it. That is why an
 * undocumented parameter is safe to rely on here: when it does nothing, the
 * result is an empty chat and a full clipboard, which is the fallback anyway.
 */
const CHATS = [
  { id: 'chatgpt', name: 'ChatGPT', href: 'https://chatgpt.com/?q=' },
  { id: 'claude', name: 'Claude', href: 'https://claude.ai/new?q=' },
  { id: 'gemini', name: 'Gemini', href: 'https://gemini.google.com/app?q=' },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Redazione"
  description="Come preparare una news o la scheda di un sito con l'aiuto di un'intelligenza artificiale, rispettando le regole del sito."
  intro="Le regole della casa in un blocco di testo da incollare in ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude o quello che usi. Funziona anche con gli account gratuiti."
  noindex
>
  <div class="prose">
    <H2>Come funziona</H2>

    <ol>
      <li>Dì qui sotto quale intelligenza artificiale usi. Il sito se lo ricorda.</li>
      <li>
        Scegli cosa devi scrivere e premi il pulsante: copia il prompt e apre la chat.
        Se l'intelligenza artificiale non riesce ad aprire da sola le istruzioni, te lo
        dice: a quel punto incolla, ce le hai già negli appunti.
      </li>
      <li>
        Nella stessa finestra, sotto, scrivi le informazioni che hai: appunti, il
        messaggio che ti è arrivato, quello che c'è sulla locandina. Anche disordinate,
        non importa.
      </li>
      <li>
        Ricevi il valore di ogni campo, il testo da incollare e l'elenco di quello che
        manca. Poi apri <a href={keystatic}>il gestore dei contenuti</a> e riempi i campi.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      <strong>Rileggi sempre prima di pubblicare.</strong> Un'intelligenza artificiale sbaglia
      le date e si inventa i dettagli con la faccia di chi li sa. Le istruzioni le dicono
      di lasciare vuoto quello che non sa e di elencartelo, ma l'ultima parola è tua: controlla
      date, luoghi e quote contro i tuoi appunti. Niente va online prima che tu unisca le
      modifiche.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="picker field">
    <label for="ai">Quale intelligenza artificiale usi?</label>
    <select id="ai">
      <option value="copia">Nessuna, copio soltanto</option>
      {
        CHATS.map((chat) => (
          <option value={chat.id} data-url={chat.href}>
            {chat.name}
          </option>
        ))
      }
    </select>
    <noscript>
      Senza JavaScript i pulsanti non funzionano: seleziona il testo nella casella e
      copialo a mano.
    </noscript>
  </div>

  {
    blocks.map(({ kind, label, hint, text, short }) => (
      <section class="kit">
        <H2 id={`prompt-${kind}`}>{label}</H2>
        <p class="kit__hint">{hint}</p>

        <div class="field">
          <label class="visually-hidden" for={`testo-${kind}`}>
            Il prompt da copiare
          </label>
          {/* `set:text` rather than a child: an expression inside the tag would
              put the JSX indentation into the value the volunteer copies. */}
          <textarea
            class="kit__text"
            readonly
            rows="10"
            id={`testo-${kind}`}
            set:text={text}
          />
        </div>

        <p class="kit__actions">
          <button
            class="button"
            type="button"
            data-copy={`testo-${kind}`}
            data-short={short}
          >
            Copia il prompt
          </button>
        </p>
      </section>
    ))
  }

  <div class="prose">
    <H2>Le istruzioni da sole</H2>
    <p>
      Il prompt qui sopra contiene già tutto. Se ti serve leggere le regole senza il
      resto, o passarle a qualcuno, sono qui: <a href={plainText}>istruzioni.txt</a>. Le
      stesse tabelle in forma di dati, per chi collega il sito a un programma:
      <a href={asJson}>contenuti.json</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<script>
  /*
    One remembered choice, one button per prompt.

    The date is prepended here rather than baked into the page because the page
    is built once and read for months: a model told the wrong date will happily
    decide next Saturday's event already happened. `Intl` gives the Italian
    spelling for free.

    No import, so Astro keeps this inline: on a page nobody but an editor opens
    it would still be an extra request. See AGENTS.md, known traps.
  */
  const KEY = 'redazione-ai';

  const today = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('it-IT', { dateStyle: 'full' }).format(
    new Date(),
  );

  const select = document.getElementById('ai') as HTMLSelectElement;
  const buttons = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-copy]')];

  const chosen = () => select.options[select.selectedIndex];

  /* Both directions wrapped: a browser set to block site data throws on read
     as well as on write, and losing a preference must not break the button. */
  const remember = (value: string) => {
    try {
      localStorage.setItem(KEY, value);
    } catch {
      /* Private mode, or site data blocked. The choice lasts this visit. */
    }
  };

  const label = () => {
    const chat = chosen();
    const text =
      chat.value === 'copia' ? 'Copia il prompt' : `Copia e apri ${chat.text}`;
    for (const button of buttons) button.textContent = text;
  };

  try {
    const saved = localStorage.getItem(KEY);
    if (saved && [...select.options].some((option) => option.value === saved)) {
      select.value = saved;
    }
  } catch {
    /* Nothing saved, or nothing readable. The default option stands. */
  }

  label();

  select.addEventListener('change', () => {
    remember(select.value);
    label();
  });

  /*
    Copy, without ever throwing.

    `navigator.clipboard` does not exist on an insecure origin, and `npm run
    dev` advertises `http://192.168.x.x:4321` beside localhost. Opened at that
    address, the first line of this handler threw, so nothing was copied *and*
    no tab opened: the broken half took the working half with it.

    `execCommand` is deprecated and is the only thing that copies there. It
    works on the selection and the box does not hold the date line, so the
    value is swapped for one synchronous call and put back before anything can
    paint.
  */
  const copy = (box: HTMLTextAreaElement, text: string) => {
    try {
      if (navigator.clipboard?.writeText) return navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);

      const original = box.value;
      box.value = text;
      box.select();
      /* Cast because the deprecation is the point: `astro check` flags the
         real signature, and there is no replacement that copies on an
         insecure origin. */
      const legacy = document as unknown as { execCommand(command: string): boolean };
      const ok = legacy.execCommand('copy');
      box.value = original;
      return ok ? Promise.resolve() : Promise.reject(new Error('copy refused'));
    } catch (error) {
      return Promise.reject(error);
    }
  };

  for (const button of buttons) {
    button.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const box = document.getElementById(button.dataset.copy!) as HTMLTextAreaElement;
      const chat = chosen();

      /*
        Order matters. The copy starts first, inside the gesture and while the
        document still has focus; `window.open` follows in the same turn, so
        the popup blocker sees a real click. Awaiting the copy before opening
        would lose the gesture and the tab with it.
      */
      const copied = copy(box, `Oggi è ${today}.\n\n${box.value}`);

      if (chat.value !== 'copia') {
        const url = chat.dataset.url + encodeURIComponent(button.dataset.short!);
        window.open(url, '_blank', 'noopener');
      }

      copied
        .then(() => {
          button.textContent = 'Copiato';
          setTimeout(label, 2000);
        })
        .catch(() => {
          /* Refused, usually because the new tab took the focus. Leave the
             text selected so one keystroke still gets it. */
          box.focus();
          box.select();
          button.textContent = 'Premi Ctrl+C';
        });
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .kit {
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .kit__hint {
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .kit__text {
    /* Monospace so a stray character in a field value is visible, and small:
       this is a block to be copied, not to be read closely. */
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, monospace;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.5;
    resize: vertical;
    /* Wrap rather than scroll sideways: a soft wrap does not alter `value`,
       so what is copied is still the text as written. */
    white-space: pre-wrap;
  }

  .kit__actions {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .picker {
    max-width: var(--prose-width);
    margin-block: var(--space-lg);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--color-bg-subtle);
  }

  .picker select {
    /* `.field` styles inputs and textareas; a select is not one of them, and
       adding it there would reach into the CMS's own controls. */
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--color-bg);
    color: var(--color-text);
    font: inherit;
  }

  .picker select:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-focus);
    outline-offset: 1px;
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
  }

  .picker noscript {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
  }
</style>
